Help For The Receive Audio Setup

Choose the recording input that carries your radio audio. YAC uses the device names supplied by your system, such as USB Audio CODEC. On Windows, compare with the Recording tab in Sound settings. A name containing USB does not prove it is the radio.

Comma selects the previous input; full stop selects the next. They stop at the ends. c changes the input channel. Channel numbers do not necessarily mean Main and Sub. Browsing does not open an input or save a choice.

Enter checks the selected input format, saves the choice for YAC and returns to the decoder menu. If the check or save fails, the selection stays open. h reads this help. q leaves without saving your browsing changes. Your previous saved choice is kept.

v checks the selected channel for one second and reports its level without playing it. Receive a steady signal and compare the reading. This checks for audio, not correct decoding. Clipping means the input is too loud. An explicit check or save stops active audio CW capture; restart it with c from the decoder menu.

YAC never changes Windows defaults, screen-reader sound or speaker output. It retains your saved device choice. Missing or ambiguous inputs are reported rather than silently replaced. If no input is selected, use full stop/comma or c before checking or saving. Audio CW requires the optional audio dependency.

On Linux and Mac, this list includes FT inputs. One choice saves both decoders when YAC can match them to the same input. A choice announced as FT saves only the FT input. Channel and level controls apply to audio CW. FT uses the engine's input mix.

